<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/undated">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucid–Mauryan war</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Treaty of the Indus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seleucid–Mauryan war was the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> at the end of the fourth century BCE, which ended in a peace settlement often called the Treaty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Seleucus ceded territories in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and received a large number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> in return. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> treat the settlement as shaping the empire's eastern frontier.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucid-mauryan-war.jpg" alt="Seleucid–Mauryan war" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Jonathan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EasternSatrapsAfterAlexander_with_ceded_territories.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
